<article class="post-body">

<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Three Paradigms, One Shared Blind Spot</h2>
<p>Language models have converged on a deep dichotomy: autoregressive (AR) models predict tokens strictly left-to-right; diffusion-based models aim to break that fixed order. But the deepest question is not <em>AR vs diffusion</em> — it's <em>what level of abstraction should the model reason in?</em></p>

<div class="paradigm-grid">
  <div class="paradigm-card">
    <div class="paradigm-title">Autoregressive (AR)</div>
    <div class="paradigm-subtitle">e.g. LLaMA, GPT</div>
    <div class="paradigm-desc">Factorizes p(x) via the chain rule. Each token x<sub>i</sub> is predicted given all previous tokens. Generation is inherently sequential — inference depth scales with output length.</div>
    <div><span class="paradigm-badge badge-red">fixed left-to-right order</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="paradigm-card">
    <div class="paradigm-title">Discrete DLM</div>
    <div class="paradigm-subtitle">e.g. LLaDA, MDLM</div>
    <div class="paradigm-desc">Masks or corrupts tokens, then denoises them back in discrete space. Removes the left-to-right bias but still works token-by-token at the observation level. Intermediate states carry little global structure.</div>
    <div><span class="paradigm-badge badge-amber">observation recovery in token space</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="paradigm-card highlight">
    <div class="paradigm-title">Cola DLM</div>
    <div class="paradigm-subtitle">ByteDance Seed, 2026</div>
    <div class="paradigm-desc">Compresses text into compact <em>latent blocks</em> via a Text VAE. A block-causal DiT learns a continuous prior over those latents through flow matching. The decoder handles local token realization.</div>
    <div><span class="paradigm-badge badge-green">prior transport in latent space</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The key insight: continuous DLMs like Plaid also use continuous representations — but they still try to recover a <em>token-aligned</em> representation (the noisy embedding of the ground-truth token). Cola DLM is different: the diffusion path never touches token-level targets. It learns a <strong>prior distribution over compressed latents</strong>, and delegates all token decision-making to a conditional decoder.</p>

