<h3>Stage 1: Text VAE — building a stable interface</h3>
<p>The encoder <em>q(z&#8320; | x)</em> maps text to a continuous latent, and the decoder <em>p(x | z&#8320;)</em> reconstructs it. The objective combines reconstruction loss, a KL divergence term to a Gaussian base prior, and a BERT-style masking loss that prevents the encoder from ignoring semantics. After Stage 1, the VAE achieves near-perfect reconstruction (token accuracy &#8776; 0.9998 at zero noise).</p>

<h3>Stage 2: Block-Causal DiT — learning the latent prior</h3>
<p>Now the DiT learns <em>p&#120595;(z&#8320;)</em> — a flow matching model that transports Gaussian noise to the latent distribution. The key structure is <strong>block-causal attention</strong>: within each block of latent positions, the DiT uses bidirectional attention (can see all positions); across blocks, it's causal (can only see past blocks). This preserves prefix conditioning while enabling parallel generation within each block.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">ELBO decomposition (Eq. 3.5)</div>
  E[log p(x)] <span class="math-var">&ge;</span> E[log p_<span class="math-var">&theta;</span>(x|z&#8320;)]  <span class="math-comment">reconstruction</span><br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="math-var">&minus; I_q(X; Z&#8320;)</span>         <span class="math-comment">information compression</span><br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="math-var">&minus; KL(q&#772;&#8346;(z&#8320;) &#x2016; p&#120595;(z&#8320;))</span>  <span class="math-comment">prior matching</span>
</div>

<h3>Inference: prefix &#8594; latent blocks &#8594; tokens</h3>
<p>At inference: (1) encode the prompt into clean prefix latents; (2) generate each response latent block by running the flow from noise conditioned on prior blocks; (3) decode all latent blocks to tokens in parallel. Block size 16 means 16 tokens are decoded per sequential denoising call — yielding a 1.6–2&#215; reduction in sequential depth vs token-by-token AR generation.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-transport" data-code="transport">
<div class="section-no">03 — Prior Transport</div>
<h2>The Unified Markov-Path Perspective</h2>
<p>The paper provides a rigorous framework unifying all four paradigms. The key question: <em>what is the stochastic path doing?</em> Each method defines a Markov process, but they differ fundamentally in what state the process operates on and what role that process plays.</p>

<table class="results-table" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Method</th>
      <th>State Space</th>
      <th>Path Role</th>
      <th>Explicit Latent?</th>
      <th>Continuity Where?</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>AR</strong></td>
      <td class="mn">Prefix tokens</td>
      <td>Direct generation path</td>
      <td class="mn" style="color:var(--muted)">&#10007;</td>
      <td class="mn" style="color:var(--muted)">None</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>LLaDA</strong></td>
      <td class="mn">Discrete masked seqs</td>
      <td>Observation recovery (discrete)</td>
      <td class="mn" style="color:var(--muted)">&#10007;</td>
      <td class="mn" style="color:var(--muted)">Token space</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Plaid</strong></td>
      <td class="mn">Continuous token-aligned reps</td>
      <td>Observation recovery (continuous)</td>
      <td class="mn" style="color:var(--muted)">&#10007;</td>
      <td class="mn">Token space</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row">
      <td><strong>Cola DLM</strong></td>
      <td class="mn">Compressed latent seqs</td>
      <td><strong>Prior-transport path</strong></td>
      <td class="mn best">&#10003;</td>
      <td class="mn best">Latent space</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The crucial distinction: Plaid and other "continuous" DLMs apply flow matching to recover <em>h&#8320; = E(x)</em> — the token embedding of the ground-truth token. The path starts at a noisy version of that embedding and denoises back to it. This is still observation recovery — just in continuous space. Cola DLM's flow path never knows the target token embedding. It only transports <em>Gaussian noise to the latent distribution q&#772;&#8346;(z&#8320;)</em>. Token generation happens entirely in the decoder.</p>

