<h3>When is a latent bottleneck actually useful?</h3>
<p>The paper derives a clean answer via rate-distortion theory. A latent bottleneck helps when the data admits a global variable <em>G</em> such that <em>H(X | G) &lt;&lt; H(X)</em> — i.e., knowing the global semantics massively reduces uncertainty over tokens. In that case, Cola DLM's factorization (prior models <em>G</em>, decoder models <em>X | G</em>) matches the true data-generating process.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-latent" data-code="latent">
<div class="section-no">04 — Latent Space Design</div>
<h2>Neither Fixed Nor From Scratch</h2>
<p>The latent space must be good for two things simultaneously: the VAE must reconstruct tokens faithfully, and the latent distribution must be learnable by the DiT flow model. These two objectives can conflict, and naive strategies fail at one of them.</p>
<p>The key finding: the latent space should <strong>evolve jointly with the DiT</strong>, but must start from a <strong>stable pretrained VAE</strong> — not from random initialization. The chart below shows Task Average accuracy across the full training range for four strategies.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">latent-strategy-comparison.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a strategy to highlight its curve — Task Average % vs EFLOPs</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="infer-controls">
      <label>highlight:</label>
      <button class="infer-btn active" data-strat="all" id="strat-all">all</button>
      <button class="infer-btn" data-strat="joint" id="strat-joint">Joint DiT x1 &#9733;</button>
      <button class="infer-btn" data-strat="fix" id="strat-fix">Fix VAE</button>
      <button class="infer-btn" data-strat="interval" id="strat-interval">Interval</button>
      <button class="infer-btn" data-strat="scratch" id="strat-scratch">All Scratch</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="latent-canvas" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Joint DiT x1 (purple) accelerates steadily and reaches 32.5% at 389 EFLOPs. Fix VAE (amber) is competitive early but saturates around 25%. Both Interval and All Scratch lag throughout — the latent space needs both continuous updates and a stable initialization.</p>

<h3>Why does BERT-style masking matter?</h3>
<p>Strong latent evolution can degrade performance if the updates are not guided toward semantic structure. A BERT-style auxiliary loss — predicting masked tokens from the latent — forces the encoder to preserve recoverable semantic information in the bottleneck. At VAE learning rate ratio = 1, adding the BERT loss improves Task Average by ~4 points.</p>

<h3>Global semantic structures exist in the latent</h3>
<p>As latent dimension increases from 16 to 128, the optimal noise schedule location (loc) shifts systematically from ~1.0 to ~2.3. Under a purely local, dimension-separable latent, this drift should not occur. The fact that it does — consistently across LAMBADA, MMLU, and SIQA — is strong evidence that the latent encodes shared cross-dimensional semantic structure.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-inference" data-code="inference">
<div class="section-no">05 — Inference Levers</div>
<h2>Denoising Steps and CFG: Where to Set the Knobs</h2>
<p>Two hyperparameters control inference quality: the number of denoising steps applied to each latent block, and the Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) scale. Both have sweet spots — too few steps and the model can't recover semantics; too many and you're paying for negligible gain.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">denoising-steps-accuracy.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a step count to see accuracy — performance saturates after ~16 steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="infer-controls" id="step-controls">
      <label>steps:</label>
    </div>
    <canvas id="steps-canvas" style="width:100%;max-width:520px;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.625rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      &#9733; with block_size=16, each denoising call generates 16 tokens in parallel
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Most of the practical gain comes within the first 8–10 steps. At step 16, performance is essentially at its ceiling. Since each denoising call generates a full block of 16 tokens, Cola DLM needs far fewer <em>sequential</em> calls than an AR model for the same output length.</p>

<h3>Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG)</h3>
<p>CFG is applied to block-level conditional denoising. A CFG scale of 3–7 gives the best results — it meaningfully sharpens the conditional distribution without distorting the latent trajectory. Beyond CFG &#8776; 10, all task curves degrade sharply.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">CFG-guided denoising output</div>
  v_guided = <span class="math-var">v(z_t, t)</span>  <span class="math-comment">unconditioned</span><br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; + <span class="math-var">&omega;</span> &middot; (<span class="math-var">v(z_t, t; c) &minus; v(z_t, t)</span>)  <span class="math-comment">&omega; &#8776; 3&ndash;7 optimal</span>
</div>

<h3>Block-wise inference walkthrough</h3>
<div class="step-list" id="infer-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Encode prefix</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run VAE encoder on prompt tokens to get clean prefix latents z_pre</div>
      <div class="step-code">z_pre = vae_encoder(prompt_tokens)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sample noise seed</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each response block b, sample eps ~ N(0, I)</div>
      <div class="step-code">eps = torch.randn(B, block_size, d_latent)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
