<h3>The PPL mismatch: why likelihood is a bad metric here</h3>
<p>Cola DLM's perplexity estimates are dramatically worse than its generation quality. For the token "at", the likelihood-derived PPL improves from 1.15 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> to 641.57 as the VAE logSNR is tuned — but the generated token changes from the correct "on" to the wrong "in". Generation only requires the prior to reach a latent region from which the decoder outputs the right token; likelihood estimation requires precise local probability calibration around the gold posterior. These are different objects.</p>

<h3>What's next: latent compression and multimodal extension</h3>
<p>Cola DLM's patch-size experiments show that compressing 2 tokens into 1 latent can match or exceed 1:1 mapping — but only when prompt lengths are divisible by the patch size. With block_size=16 and patch_size=2, a single denoising call generates 32 tokens. The paper also demonstrates a preliminary text-to-image extension using a joint MMDiT, suggesting Cola DLM's probabilistic decomposition naturally extends to multimodal generation.</p>
</section>

